'''aptha''' (ap′thä̤), ''n.'' See ''aphtha.''

'''aptitude''' (ap′ti-tūd), ''n.'' [= F. ''aptitude'', < ML. ''aptitudo'', < L. ''aptus'', apt, fit: see ''apt'', ''a.'' Cf. ''attitude'', which is a doublet of ''aptitude.''] '''1.''' The state or quality of being apt or fit for or suited to a purpose, place, or situation; fitness; suitableness.
{{smaller block|''Aptitude'' . . . for the end to which it was aimed.
{{right|''Decay of Christ. Piety.''}}}}
'''2.''' A natural tendency or acquired inclination; both capacity and propensity for a certain course: as, oil has an ''aptitude'' to burn; men acquire an ''aptitude'' to particular vices.
{{smaller block|He that is about children should learn their nature and ''aptitudes.''
{{right|''Locke.''}}}}
{{smaller block|The Americans have at all times shown a remarkable}}

readings.c1s2:
''aptitude'' for the sea-faring life, and they did not wait for the Declaration of Independence to take measures for the construction of an independent navy.
{{right|''Lecky'', Eng. in 18th Cent., xiv.}}}}
'''3.''' Readiness in learning; teachableness; quickness to understand and acquire; intelligence; talent.
{{smaller block|He was a boy of remarkable ''aptitude.'' {{right|''Macaulay.''}}}}
{{smaller|='''Syn.''' ''Faculty'', ''Capacity'', etc. See ''genius''.}}

'''aptitudinal''' (ap-ti-tū′di-nạl), ''a.'' [< ML. ''aptitudo'' (''aptitudin-'') + ''-al'': see ''aptitude''.] '''1.''' Relating to an aptitude or aptitudes.—'''2.''' Existing in possibility or capacity merely. [Rare.]—{{smaller|'''Aptitudinal relation''', a relation which does not require the correlate to exist actually, but only potentially; as, for example, the relation of a desire to its object.}}

'''aptitudinally''' (ap-ti-tū′di-nạl-i), ''adv.'' In an aptitudinal manner; in a way which reveals aptitude.

'''aptly''' (apt′li), ''adv.'' In an apt or suitable manner. {{smaller|(''a'') With exact correspondence; with fitness; justly.}}
{{smaller block|<poem>I have forgot your name; but, sure, that part
Was ''aptly'' fitted, and naturally perform’d.</poem>
{{right|''Shak.'', T. of the S., Ind., i.}}}}
{{smaller|(''b'') Suitably; appropriately: of language, pertinently, appositely, or significantly.}}
{{smaller block|Irenæus very ''aptly'' remarks. {{right|''Addison.''}}}}
{{smaller block|<poem>Words ''aptly'' cull’d and meanings well express’d
Can calm the sorrows of a wounded breast.</poem>
{{right|''Crabbe'', The Village.}}}}
{{smaller|(''c'') Readily; quickly; cleverly: as, to learn ''aptly''.}}

'''aptness''' (apt′nes), ''n.'' The state or quality of being apt, in any sense of that word.
{{smaller block|The ''aptness'' of things to their end. {{right|''Hooker.''}}}}
{{smaller block|What should be the ''aptness'' of birds, in comparison of beasts, to imitate speech may be inquired. {{right|''Bacon.''}}}}

