¶'''Apulian''' (a̤-pū′li-a̤n), ''a.'' [< L. ''Apulia'', ''Appulia'', + ''-an''.] Of or pertaining to the region called Apulia, in southern Italy, or to its inhabitants. {{smaller|In Roman times Apulia included the region between the Apennines and the Adriatic, south of the Frentani and east of Samnium, and later also the Messapian peninsula. Modern Apulia comprises the provinces Foggia, Bari, and Lecce.}}
{{smaller block|A hill in the midst of the ''Apulian'' plain.
{{right|''Encyc. Brit.'', XV. 39.}}}}

{{smaller|'''Apulian pottery''', a name given to the Italo-Greek pottery found in Apulia and southeastern Italy generally, especially to the vases with red figures on a lustrous black ground, some of the most important examples of which are from this region.}}

